The Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer (BSD) Conjecture is one of the most famous and deep conjectures in number theory, connecting the algebraic properties of elliptic curves to their analytic properties through the associated L-functions. The conjecture has several parts, but one of the key aspects is the relationship between the rank of the Mordell-Weil group of an elliptic curve and the order of vanishing of its L-function at \( s = 1 \).

### Elliptic Curves and L-functions

An elliptic curve \( E \) over the rational numbers \( \mathbb{Q} \) can be given by a Weierstrass equation:
\[ E: y^2 = x^3 + ax + b \]
where \( a \) and \( b \) are integers. The set of rational points on \( E \), denoted \( E(\mathbb{Q}) \), forms a finitely generated abelian group according to the Mordell-Weil theorem:
\[ E(\mathbb{Q}) \cong \mathbb{Z}^r \oplus T \]
where \( r \) is the rank of the curve and \( T \) is the torsion subgroup.

### L-function of an Elliptic Curve

The L-function associated with \( E \) is defined as:
\[ L(E, s) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} \frac{a_n}{n^s} \]
where \( a_n \) are the Fourier coefficients related to the Hasse-Weil L-series. This L-function can be analytically continued to the entire complex plane and satisfies a functional equation.

